---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
---
<LessonLayout title="Workbook — Anointing of the Sick" cic="CCC 1499-1532" scripture="James 5:14-15">

  <section class="content">
    <h2>1. Recalling the Essentials</h2>
    <p>Briefly answer the following questions based on the lesson:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Which saint is mentioned in the New Testament as the one who commands the anointing of the sick?</li>
      <li>What are the three necessary elements for this sacrament according to the Letter of James?</li>
      <li>Is the Anointing of the Sick only for those who are about to die immediately? Explain why.</li>
      <li>Mention two spiritual effects of receiving the Anointing.</li>
      <li>What is "Viaticum" and when is it received?</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>2. For Reflection and Writing</h2>
    <p>Take a moment of silence and respond truthfully:</p>
    
    <div class="question-block">
      <p class="question-block__q">When you get sick or suffer physical pain, what is usually your first reaction toward God?</p>
      <p class="question-block__p">__________________________________________________________________________________________</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question-block">
      <p class="question-block__q">Who among your family or friends could you explain the meaning of Anointing to today so they aren't afraid of it?</p>
      <p class="question-block__p">__________________________________________________________________________________________</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question-block">
      <p class="question-block__q">What does it mean to you that Jesus is the "Good Samaritan" who heals your wounds?</p>
      <p class="question-block__p">__________________________________________________________________________________________</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question-block">
      <p class="question-block__q">How do you believe the Holy Spirit can give you "peace and courage" in the midst of a difficult trial?</p>
      <p class="question-block__p">__________________________________________________________________________________________</p>
    </div>

    <div class="question-block">
      <p class="question-block__q">Write a short prayer asking for the sick who suffer alone in hospitals.</p>
      <p class="question-block__p">__________________________________________________________________________________________</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>3. Practical Activities</h2>
    <div class="activity-box">
      <p><strong>Visit or Call:</strong> Identify a sick or elderly person in your community. This week, call or visit them. You don't need to say much, just let them know that God is with them. If appropriate, mention the beauty of the sacrament of Anointing.</p>
    </div>
    <br />
    <div class="activity-box">
      <p><strong>Deep Reading:</strong> Read the passage from James 5:14-15 in your Bible and underline the words that give you the most comfort. Close your eyes and imagine Jesus touching your own wounds or weaknesses.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>4. Verse to Memorize</h2>
    <div class="bible-passage">
      <p>"Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord."</p>
      <p class="bible-passage__ref">— James 5:14</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content">
    <h2>5. Prayer for This Week</h2>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Lord Jesus, Physician of souls,<br />
        You who know the weight of pain and weakness,<br />
        be present in my life and in the lives of my loved ones.<br />
        Give us the grace to trust in Your Fatherly love<br />
        when our strength fails.<br />
        May Your Holy Spirit fill us with peace and fortitude,<br />
        and may we never forget that we are Your beloved children.<br />
        Amen.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <div class="downloads">
    <a href="javascript:window.print()" class="download-btn">Print Workbook</a>
  </div>

</LessonLayout>